      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve for \\(x + y\\) given the equation \\(x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34\\), we start by rearranging the equation to bring all terms to one side:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 - 10x + 6y + 34 = 0\n\\]\n\nNext, we complete the square for both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). For the \\(x\\)-terms, \\(x^2 - 10x\\), we add and subtract \\((10/2)^2 = 25\\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x = (x - 5)^2 - 25\n\\]\n\nFor the \\(y\\)-terms, \\(y^2 + 6y\\), we add and subtract \\((6/2)^2 = 9\\):\n\n\\[\ny^2 + 6y = (y + 3)^2 - 9\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these completed squares back into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 - 25 + (y + 3)^2 - 9 + 34 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the constants on the left side:\n\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 - 10 = 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 10\n\\]\n\nThis is the equation of a circle with center \\((5, -3)\\) and radius \\(\\sqrt{10}\\). To find the points of intersection of this circle with the line \\(x + y = k\\), we substitute \\(y = k - x\\) into the circle's equation:\n\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 + ((k - x) + 3)^2 = 10\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the equation:\n\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 + (k - x + 3)^2 = 10\n\\]\n\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 + (k - x + 3)^2 = 10\n\\]\n\nExpanding the terms:\n\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 = x^2 - 10x + 25\n\\]\n\\[\n(k - x + 3)^2 = k^2 - 2kx + x^2 - 6k + 9\n\\]\n\nCombining these:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x + 25 + k^2 - 2kx + x^2 - 6k + 9 = 10\n\\]\n\n\\[\n2x^2 - (10 + 2k)x + (25 + k^2 - 6k + 9) = 10\n\\]\n\n\\[\n2x^2 - (10 + 2k)x + (k^2 - 6k + 34) = 10\n\\]\n\n\\[\n2x^2 - (10 + 2k)x + (k^2 - 6k + 24) = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(x\\). For \\(x\\) to be real, the discriminant must be non-negative. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of the quadratic equation \\(2x^2 - (10 + 2k)x + (k^2 - 6k + 24) = 0\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac = (10 + 2k)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (k^2 - 6k + 24)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 100 + 40k + 4k^2 - 8(k^2 - 6k + 24)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 100 + 40k + 4k^2 - 8k^2 + 48k - 192\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = -4k^2 + 88k - 92\n\\]\n\nFor the discriminant to be non-negative:\n\n\\[\n-4k^2 + 88k - 92 \\geq 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\nk^2 - 22k + 23 \\leq 0\n\\]\n\nSolving the quadratic inequality \\(k^2 - 22k + 23 \\leq 0\\), we find the roots of the quadratic equation \\(k^2 - 22k + 23 = 0\\):\n\n\\[\nk = \\frac{22 \\pm \\sqrt{484 - 92}}{2} = \\frac{22 \\pm \\sqrt{392}}{2} = \\frac{22 \\pm 14\\sqrt{2}}{2} = 11 \\pm 7\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the roots are \\(k = 11 + 7\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(k = 11 - 7\\sqrt{2}\\). The quadratic \\(k^2 - 22k + 23 \\leq 0\\) is satisfied for \\(k\\) in the interval \\([11 - 7\\sqrt{2}, 11 + 7\\sqrt{2}]\\).\n\nTo find \\(x + y\\), we use the fact that the center of the circle is \\((5, -3)\\) and the line \\(x + y = k\\) is tangent to the circle. The distance from the center \\((5, -3)\\) to the line \\(x + y = k\\) is equal to the radius \\(\\sqrt{10}\\). The distance from a point \\((x_1, y_1)\\) to a line \\(Ax + By + C = 0\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|Ax_1 + By_1 + C|}{\\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}}\n\\]\n\nFor the line \\(x + y - k = 0\\), the distance from \\((5, -3)\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|5 + (-3) - k|}{\\sqrt{1^2 + 1^2}} = \\frac{|2 - k|}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\sqrt{10}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n|2 - k| = \\sqrt{20} = 2\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(2 - k = 2\\sqrt{5}\\) or \\(2 - k = -2\\sqrt{5}\\). Solving these, we get:\n\n\\[\nk = 2 - 2\\sqrt{5} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad k = 2 + 2\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) must be in the interval \\([11 - 7\\sqrt{2}, 11 + 7\\sqrt{2}]\\), we check which value is within this interval. We find that \\(11 - 7\\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.11\\) and \\(11 + 7\\sqrt{2} \\approx 19.89\\), and \\(2 - 2\\sqrt{5} \\approx -0.82\\) and \\(2 + 2\\sqrt{5} \\approx 5.83\\), so the only valid solution is \\(k = 2 + 2\\sqrt{5}\\).\n\nThus, \\(x + y = 2\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
